"] [:db/cas 17592186058900 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2})^{27}" "\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{27}"] [:db/cas 17592186058901 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2})^{6}" "\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{6}"] [:db/cas 17592186060379 :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{3}$?" "What is $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{3} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{3}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186058921 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2})^{36}" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{36}"] [:db/cas 17592186058922 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16})^{13}" "\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)^{13}"] [:db/cas 17592186058923 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16})^{36}" "\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)^{36}"] [:db/cas 17592186058924 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4})^{-5}" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{-5}"] [:db/cas 17592186058925 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4})^{13}" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{13}"] [:db/cas 17592186060383 :question/problem "What is $(-\\frac{1}{4})^{9} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})^{4}$?" "What is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{9} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{4}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186059442 :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059475 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\div \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\div  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\div \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}\\right) \\div (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\div  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059483 :question/worked-solution "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A$:\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\frac{b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2}}{2bc}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\quad b=7,\\quad c=12\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos A &= (7^{2} + 12^{2} - 9^{2})/(2(7)(12)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{49 + 144 - 81}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{112}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= \\arccos (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= 48.19^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A$:\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\frac{b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2}}{2bc}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\quad b=7,\\quad c=12\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos A &= (7^{2} + 12^{2} - 9^{2})/(2(7)(12)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{49 + 144 - 81}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{112}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= \\arccos \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= 48.19^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186059487 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$" "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059493 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u\\cos v - \\cos u\\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=30^{\\circ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u\\cos v - \\cos u\\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=30^{\\circ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059494 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(x-y) = \\sin x\\cos y - \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(x-y) = \\sin x\\cos y - \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059495 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(x-y) = \\dfrac{\\tan{x}-\\tan{y}}{1+\\tan{x}\\tan{y}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\frac{π}{12}) &= \\tan (\\frac{π}{3} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= (\\tan (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\tan (\\frac{π}{4}))/(1 + \\tan (\\frac{π}{3})\\tan (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\frac{π}{12}) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3} + (-1)^{2})/((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(x-y) = \\dfrac{\\tan{x}-\\tan{y}}{1+\\tan{x}\\tan{y}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right) &= \\tan (\\frac{π}{3} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= (\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right) - \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(1 + \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3} + (-1)^{2})/((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059496 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059497 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059498 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u-v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}-\\tan{v}}{1+\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with $u=x$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\tan x - \\tan (\\frac{π}{4}))/(1 + \\tan x\\tan (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{1 + \\tan x \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{\\tan x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u-v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}-\\tan{v}}{1+\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with $u=x$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\tan x - \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(1 + \\tan x\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{1 + \\tan x \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{\\tan x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059500 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)]\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059502 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059506 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =3(4){x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3){x}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =3(4){x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3){x}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059508 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the cofunction of $\\cos x$ (**co**sine) is $\\sin x$ (sine). The corresponding identity is given by\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - x \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos(\\frac{π}{5}) &= \\sin(\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{3π}{10})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 5\\right)} = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {10}\\right)\n$$" "First, notice that the cofunction of $\\cos x$ (**co**sine) is $\\sin x$ (sine). The corresponding identity is given by\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - x \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right) &= \\sin(\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\sin \\left(\\frac{3π}{10}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 5\\right)} = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {10}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059529 :question/worked-solution "We want the angle $\\theta$, as shown below. Note that the vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/50/501f85a2a6d6d69e737cd507c542869b12494c3ce4979212e806ad223f89447a.png)\n\nApplying the formula for the angle that a vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{a_{y}}{a_{x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{-2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan (-\\frac{3}{2}) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 56.3^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $2$nd quadrant, so we want a solution $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\n\nTo get a solution in the desired range, we choose $n=1$ and get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 56.3^{∘} + 180^{∘}; = 123.7^{∘}\n$$" "We want the angle $\\theta$, as shown below. Note that the vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/50/501f85a2a6d6d69e737cd507c542869b12494c3ce4979212e806ad223f89447a.png)\n\nApplying the formula for the angle that a vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{a_{y}}{a_{x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{-2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 56.3^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $2$nd quadrant, so we want a solution $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\n\nTo get a solution in the desired range, we choose $n=1$ and get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 56.3^{∘} + 180^{∘}; = 123.7^{∘}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059531 :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b3d4d12313dcbb5d882ce547af97037694ff6e12fd134c7f9fd1243ac2bdc1e.png)\n\nFirst, we need to find the angle $\\theta$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $x$-axis. Applying the usual formula, we obtain the equation\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{a_y}{a_x} = \\dfrac{-1}{2} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan (-\\frac{1}{2}) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 26.6^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $4$th quadrant. Choosing $n=0$, we get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 26.6^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the angle $\\theta'$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $y$-axis, we add $90^\\circ$ to the absolute value of the angle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^{′} &= \\mid θ \\mid + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 26.6^{∘} + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 116.6^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/9b/9b3d4d12313dcbb5d882ce547af97037694ff6e12fd134c7f9fd1243ac2bdc1e.png)\n\nFirst, we need to find the angle $\\theta$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $x$-axis. Applying the usual formula, we obtain the equation\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{a_y}{a_x} = \\dfrac{-1}{2} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 26.6^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $4$th quadrant. Choosing $n=0$, we get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 26.6^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the angle $\\theta'$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $y$-axis, we add $90^\\circ$ to the absolute value of the angle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^{′} &= \\mid θ \\mid + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 26.6^{∘} + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 116.6^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059534 :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{x} &= \\mid a\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ = 60\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\ = 60 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ a_{y}|=\\end{vmatrix}a \\mid \\sin θ \\\\\n&= 60\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{x} &= \\mid a\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ = 60\\cos \\left(\\frac{5π}{6}\\right) \\\\ = 60 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ a_{y}|=\\end{vmatrix}a \\mid \\sin θ \\\\\n&= 60\\sin \\left(\\frac{5π}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059540 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cos 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cos 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059542 :question/worked-solution "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059627 :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060099 :question/problem "What is $\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4})$?" "What is $\\cos \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060100 :question/problem "What is $\\tan (\\frac{5π}{6})$?" "What is $\\tan \\left(\\frac{5π}{6}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060101 :question/problem "What is $\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3})$?" "What is $\\sin \\left(\\frac{4π}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060103 :question/problem "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$*" "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186060103 :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060115 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\cos (\\frac{7π}{12})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{7π}{12} = \\frac{π}{3} + \\frac{π}{4}$.*" "Find the exact value of $\\cos \\left(\\frac{7π}{12}\\right)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{7π}{12} = \\frac{π}{3} + \\frac{π}{4}$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186060117 :question/problem "What is the exact value of $\\tan (\\frac{5π}{12})$?\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{5π}{12} = \\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{π}{6}$.*" "What is the exact value of $\\tan \\left(\\frac{5π}{12}\\right)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{5π}{12} = \\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{π}{6}$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186060120 :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={120}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={120}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060224 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\csc (\\frac{3π}{2})$." "Find the value of $\\csc \\left(\\frac{3π}{2}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186060241 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=$" "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186060241 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288468 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288649 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288873 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288888 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288926 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186060246 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060247 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186060247 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060249 :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2}.$" "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060249 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})]-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =1-(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)]-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060250 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186060250 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060281 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060314 :question/worked-solution "If the number of incomplete topics decreased by $\\frac{1}{6}$ every week, then every week $\\frac{5}{6}$ of the topics remained incomplete.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ week, $12\\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, approximately  $12\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, approximately $12\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}$ topics were incomplete." "If the number of incomplete topics decreased by $\\frac{1}{6}$ every week, then every week $\\frac{5}{6}$ of the topics remained incomplete.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ week, $12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, approximately  $12\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2}$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, approximately $12\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{3}$ topics were incomplete."] [:db/cas 17592186538319 :answer/value "12\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}" "12\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186538550 :answer/value "{12}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})" "{12}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186538667 :answer/value "12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}" "12\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186538737 :answer/value "{12}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})" "{12}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186538782 :answer/value "12\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{6})}^{3}" "12\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186060338 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan (\\frac{3π}{2})$." "Find the value of $\\tan \\left(\\frac{3π}{2}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186060340 :question/problem "What is $\\cot (\\frac{π}{3})$?" "What is $\\cot \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060341 :question/problem "What is $\\sec (\\frac{2π}{3})$?" "What is $\\sec \\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186060342 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to $-{45}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png)" "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to $-{45}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186060352 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={45}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={45}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060371 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-2x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060374 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})(-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060376 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060435 :question/problem "What is $\\arccos (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$?" "What is $\\arccos \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186063237 :answer/value "6 - 10\\ln (\\frac{5}{3})" "6 - 10\\ln \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186063238 :answer/value "2 + 5\\ln (\\frac{3}{4})" "2 + 5\\ln \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186063239 :answer/value "-6 + \\ln (\\frac{3}{5})" "-6 + \\ln \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186063256 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2} - \\ln (\\frac{2}{3})" "\\frac{5}{2} - \\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186063307 :answer/value "3\\arctan (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" "3\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063308 :answer/value "(3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(7) + C" "(3\\arcsin \\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right))/(7) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063310 :answer/value "(\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(3) + C" "(\\arcsin \\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right))/(3) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063311 :answer/value "3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" "3\\arcsin \\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063313 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063314 :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" "\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063315 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" "\\frac{1}{9}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063316 :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{x}{3}) + C" "\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063317 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{3}) + C" "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186063669 :answer/value "\\log_{3} (\\frac{1}{2}) = 9" "\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 9"] [:db/cas 17592186063670 :answer/value "\\log_{9} (\\frac{1}{2}) = 3" "\\log_{9} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 3"] [:db/cas 17592186063673 :answer/value "\\log_{2} (\\frac{1}{16}) = 2" "\\log_{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right) = 2"] [:db/cas 17592186063676 :answer/value "\\log_{4} (\\frac{1}{16}) =-2" "\\log_{4} \\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right) =-2"] [:db/cas 17592186063680 :answer/value "\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{5}) = 1" "\\log_{5} \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 1"] [:db/cas 17592186064937 :question/problem "$(\\frac{1}{5})^{-1} = 5$ is equivalent to" "$\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{-1} = 5$ is equivalent to"] [:db/cas 17592186063688 :answer/value "\\log_{2.154} (\\frac{1}{3}) \\approx 10" "\\log_{2.154} \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\approx 10"] [:db/cas 17592186063933 :question/worked-solution "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = lim_(h → 0)(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= lim_(h → 0)((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = lim_(h → 0)(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= lim_(h → 0)((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\left(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\left(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063934 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(x → 2)(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$." "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(x → 2)(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)\\left(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}\\right) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$."] [:db/cas 17592186063947 :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{7}{3}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7}) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number." "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)} & =\\frac{1}{4}÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{7}{3}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)} & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number."] [:db/cas 17592186063948 :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-3)}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}=-3÷(-\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $-3 =- \\dfrac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) &= (-\\frac{3}{1})÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\ &= (-\\frac{3}{1})\\times (-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\ &= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number." "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-3)}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=-3÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $-3 =- \\dfrac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{1}\\right)÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{1}\\right)\\times \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\ &= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number."] [:db/cas 17592186063949 :question/worked-solution "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{3}{2})÷6 &= (-\\frac{3}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)÷6 &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063959 :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\dfrac{11}{7}$ from $\\dfrac{3}{7},$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}-(-\\frac{11}{7}) & =\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $7,$ so we combine numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{3+11}{7}=\\frac{14}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can simplify the resulting fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{7} & =14÷7=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator first and *then* add or subtract!" "To subtract $-\\dfrac{11}{7}$ from $\\dfrac{3}{7},$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}-\\left(-\\frac{11}{7}\\right) & =\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $7,$ so we combine numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{3+11}{7}=\\frac{14}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can simplify the resulting fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{7} & =14÷7=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator first and *then* add or subtract!"] [:db/cas 17592186063962 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063968 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2,$ and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot x\\cdot 2\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{2x}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|2x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2|x|}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2,$ and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot x\\cdot 2\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2x}{y}\\right)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2x}{y}\\right)}^{2}} & =|\\frac{2x}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|2x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2|x|}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063986 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) &= (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) \\\\ &= -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order." "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) &= (-6{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\right)+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) \\\\ &= -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order."] [:db/cas 17592186063999 :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064011 :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and using the formula for the derivative of $\\arccos x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the product rule and using the formula for the derivative of $\\arccos x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064018 :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064019 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064024 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A = \\pi r^2,$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA =  \\pi r^2 =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\pi  \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A = \\pi r^2,$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA =  \\pi r^2 =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\pi  \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064029 :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69402f16f81a6653e1fdd313c314ac72db7f5514501af1ac147b5e499b305233.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between María and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between Maria and the kite.\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm dt} = -5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}$ (negative because the distance between Maria and the kite is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot (-5) = -2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $2.5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}.$" "![](images/69/69402f16f81a6653e1fdd313c314ac72db7f5514501af1ac147b5e499b305233.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between María and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between Maria and the kite.\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm dt} = -5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}$ (negative because the distance between Maria and the kite is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot (-5) = -2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $2.5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064030 :question/worked-solution "![](images/04/04784c267488ef2073c526aed683e86ca0a00d00af64eb994ce596c0cce629d9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Yurii. The quantity $z=45$ meters does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{45} \\\\ y & =45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =45(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate that the kite is losing altitude is $\\dfrac{3\\pi\\sqrt3}{2} \\,\\text{m/s}.$" "![](images/04/04784c267488ef2073c526aed683e86ca0a00d00af64eb994ce596c0cce629d9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Yurii. The quantity $z=45$ meters does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{45} \\\\ y & =45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =45\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate that the kite is losing altitude is $\\dfrac{3\\pi\\sqrt3}{2} \\,\\text{m/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064031 :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{1}{32} \\,\\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ when $r = 3 \\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is a quarter of its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{4}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}r\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{12}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{32}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{72} \\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{hr}.$" "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{1}{32} \\,\\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ when $r = 3 \\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is a quarter of its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{4}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}r\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{12}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{32}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{72} \\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{hr}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064032 :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the flask. We call $H=20\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical flask, and $R=10\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the chemical level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the flask to the surface of the chemical, $r$ the radius of the flask at the chemical surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the flask.  All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = -2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the flask to the chemical surface decreases as the chemist pours the chemical into the flask. \n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume being poured into the flask is changing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, we wish to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt}$ when the level of the chemical is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-4 \\\\  & =20-4 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the chemical must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {(16)}^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of chemical is increasing at a rate of $128\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{min}.$" "First, let's consider the dimensions of the flask. We call $H=20\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical flask, and $R=10\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the chemical level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the flask to the surface of the chemical, $r$ the radius of the flask at the chemical surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the flask.  All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = -2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the flask to the chemical surface decreases as the chemist pours the chemical into the flask. \n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume being poured into the flask is changing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, we wish to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt}$ when the level of the chemical is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-4 \\\\  & =20-4 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the chemical must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{h}{2}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {(16)}^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of chemical is increasing at a rate of $128\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{min}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064038 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\ &= {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\ &= {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064039 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064069 :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{-2}-{1}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{-2}-{1}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064075 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064080 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1-2x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x = \\dfrac 1 2 (1-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x(1-2x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}(1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}({u}^{5}-{u}^{6})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{7}{u}^{7})|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}([0-0]-[\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{42}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{168}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u=1-2x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x = \\dfrac 1 2 (1-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x(1-2x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}(1-u)\\cdot {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{0}({u}^{5}-{u}^{6})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{7}{u}^{7})|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}([0-0]-[\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{42}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{168}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064094 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064098 :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064099 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064102 :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2y - \\dfrac 1 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}y+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{3})}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{6y − 1}{3} &= \\dfrac{6y}{3} - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{6}{3} \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot y - \\dfrac 1 3 \\\\ &= 2y - \\dfrac 1 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ay+b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(2)}_{a}y+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064103 :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{4})}_{p}+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{1}{2})}_{q}a+\\underbrace{(4)}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$" "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{1 − 2a + 16b}{4} &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2a} {4} + \\dfrac {16b}{4} \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {2} {4} \\cdot a + \\dfrac {16}{4} \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {1} {2} \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot b \\\\ &= \\dfrac 1 4 − \\dfrac {a} {2} + 4b \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}a + 4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $p+qa+rb,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}_{p}+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}_{q}a+\\underbrace{(4)}_{r}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and $r=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064105 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db3fe957ac2c6a97f4dc6754de8d.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{1}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(f(x) - g(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{3} + x^{2} + 1) - (x^{2} + x + 1))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.(\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{-1}^{0}; = 0 - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}); = 0 - (-\\frac{1}{4}); = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{2}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(g(x) - f(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{2} + x + 1) - (x^{3} + x^{2} + 1))dx; =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.- (\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{0}^{1}; = - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}) + 0; = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db3fe957ac2c6a97f4dc6754de8d.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{1}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(f(x) - g(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{3} + x^{2} + 1) - (x^{2} + x + 1))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.(\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{-1}^{0}; = 0 - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}); = 0 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right); = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$A_{2}|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(g(x) - f(x))dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}((x^{2} + x + 1) - (x^{3} + x^{2} + 1))dx; =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - x)dx; = \\left.- (\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{0}^{1}; = - (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}) + 0; = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064117 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064128 :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second." "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second."] [:db/cas 17592186064133 :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. We call $H=3\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the tank, and $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4h}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{4h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi ({2}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{64}{9}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{27}{32}\\,\\text{m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of  $\\dfrac{27}{32}\\, \\text{m/min}.$" "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. We call $H=3\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the tank, and $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4h}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{4h}{3}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi ({2}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{64}{9}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{27}{32}\\,\\text{m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of  $\\dfrac{27}{32}\\, \\text{m/min}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064147 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+5)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3(x+5)-6(x+3)}{(x+3)(x+5)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{-3x-3}{(x+3)(x+5)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3(x+1)(x+3)^{2}}{(x+5)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{3(x+1)}.\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+5)^{6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+5)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\dfrac{(x+3)^3}{(x+5)^6}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}(\\frac{3}{x+3}-\\frac{6}{x+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\left(\\frac{3(x+5)-6(x+3)}{(x+3)(x+5)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{(x+5)^{6}}\\left(\\frac{-3x-3}{(x+3)(x+5)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3(x+1)(x+3)^{2}}{(x+5)^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{3(x+1)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064148 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2/3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}+1}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2({x}^{5}+1)-5{x}^{5}}{x({x}^{5}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{x({x}^{5}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}({x}^{5}+1)^{3}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x({x}^{5}+1)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-3x^5+2}.\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2/3}}{({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2/3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{5}+1)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}+1}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y =  \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{x^2}{x^5+ 1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{2({x}^{5}+1)-5{x}^{5}}{x({x}^{5}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{x({x}^{5}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}+1}}\\left(\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}({x}^{5}+1)^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{5}+2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x({x}^{5}+1)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-3x^5+2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064480 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064494 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${p}^{2},81,$ and ${q}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{{9}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot 9\\cdot q\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot q\\cdot 9\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{9q}\\cdot \\frac{p}{9q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{p}{9q})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{p}{9q})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{p}{9q}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9|\\cdot |q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${p}^{2},81,$ and ${q}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{{9}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot 9\\cdot q\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot q\\cdot 9\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{9q}\\cdot \\frac{p}{9q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{p}{9q}\\right)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{p}{9q}\\right)}^{2}} & =|\\frac{p}{9q}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9|\\cdot |q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064515 :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Calculate $F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064515 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064520 :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064544 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064572 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/ae/aef1d6e33213def71ada4a59f7d79b9f045083b2c501a752e81994e33f100277.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/ae/aef1d6e33213def71ada4a59f7d79b9f045083b2c501a752e81994e33f100277.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186064599 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) &= 2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{4})+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ &= (-\\frac{1}{2})({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+(-\\frac{2}{3}){b}^{3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} \\\\ &= 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) &= 2{b}^{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{b}{4}\\right)+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right){b}^{3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} \\\\ &= 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064607 :question/problem "What is $((\\frac{2}{5}))/((\\frac{8}{5}))$?" "What is $(\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064608 :question/problem "What is $((-9))/((\\frac{1}{2}))$?" "What is $((-9))/(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064611 :question/problem "Calculate the value of ${(\\frac{9}{8})}^{0}.$" "Calculate the value of ${\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)}^{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064611 :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{9}{8})}^{0}=1.\n$$" "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)}^{0}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064612 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$”; “The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$” only.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$”; “The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$” only.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186064630 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=7t$ be the rate at which the cars enter the traffic jam, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of cars in the traffic jam after $T$ minutes.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={60+(\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =60+\\frac{7{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=60+\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$" "Let $f(t)=7t$ be the rate at which the cars enter the traffic jam, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of cars in the traffic jam after $T$ minutes.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={60+\\left(\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =60+\\frac{7{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=60+\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064708 :question/problem "What is $\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{25})$?" "What is $\\log_{5} \\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064765 :question/problem "What is the value of $-\\frac{5}{3} + (-\\frac{2}{3})$?" "What is the value of $-\\frac{5}{3} + \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064766 :question/problem "What is $1 + (-\\frac{5}{2})$?" "What is $1 + \\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064773 :question/problem "What is $-\\frac{2}{5} - (-\\frac{6}{5})$?" "What is $-\\frac{2}{5} - \\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064775 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{1}{6} - (-\\frac{2}{3})$?" "What is $\\frac{1}{6} - \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064776 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=$" "$\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186064776 :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=3÷\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $3=\\frac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{7}{3}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 7} \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{(\\frac{7}{3})}=\\frac{9}{7}.\n$$" "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}=3÷\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $3=\\frac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{7}{3}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 7} \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{9}{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064777 :question/problem "What is $((\\frac{8}{5}))/(2)$?" "What is $(\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right))/(2)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064778 :question/problem "What is $((-\\frac{7}{2}))/(5)$?" "What is $(\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))/(5)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064780 :question/problem "What is $((-\\frac{2}{5}))/((-5))$?" "What is $(\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right))/((-5))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064781 :question/problem "What is $(7)/((-\\frac{1}{2}))$?" "What is $(7)/(\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064859 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064868 :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $5$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n|  | $5$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =5-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-\\frac{7}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-7{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5x{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|{|}_{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5(2-0)-\\frac{7}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 2+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 0+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[10-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5] \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $5$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n|  | $5$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =5-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-\\frac{7}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-7{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5x{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|{|}_{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5(2-0)-\\frac{7}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 2+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 0+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[10-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5] \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064875 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/49)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/49}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{7})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{7}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=7\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (7\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/49)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/49}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{7}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=7\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (7\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186330305 :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C" "3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186331310 :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})}{7}+C" "\\frac{3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)}{7}+C"] [:db/cas 17592186331471 :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C" "3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186332026 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})}{3}+C" "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)}{3}+C"] [:db/cas 17592186064876 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186330078 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186330281 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186330506 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186330735 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186330753 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186064877 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]+[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0]+[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]+[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0]+[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064878 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-{\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{2}{9}[{e}^{3(1/3)}-{e}^{3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{e}{3}-0]-\\frac{2}{9}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{9}-\\frac{2e}{9}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-{\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{2}{9}[{e}^{3(1/3)}-{e}^{3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{e}{3}-0]-\\frac{2}{9}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{9}-\\frac{2e}{9}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064905 :question/worked-solution "![](images/9c/9cb94ea5cab43d48da6b836026d302504d7e63fe1260aa81a2b577436b6b4e98.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between the landing point and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the drone, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ (negative because the distance between the landing point and the drone is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-6)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" "![](images/9c/9cb94ea5cab43d48da6b836026d302504d7e63fe1260aa81a2b577436b6b4e98.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between the landing point and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the drone, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ (negative because the distance between the landing point and the drone is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-6)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064925 :question/problem "What is $((-\\frac{6}{15}))/((-\\frac{12}{9}))$?" "What is $(\\left(-\\frac{6}{15}\\right))/(\\left(-\\frac{12}{9}\\right))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064926 :question/problem "What is $((-\\frac{3}{2}))/((\\frac{2}{5}))$?" "What is $(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right))$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064928 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{100}{124})}^{0}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{100}{124}\\right)}^{0}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186064928 :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{100}{124})}^{0}=1.\n$$" "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{100}{124}\\right)}^{0}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064946 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5t-10}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{5t-10}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186064964 :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{r}{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{r}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186065578 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{4}) + C" "\\frac{1}{8}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{2x - 3}{4}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186065579 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{2x - 3}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186065580 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{2x - 3}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066857 :answer/value "\\arccos (\\frac{x - 3}{2}) + C" "\\arccos \\left(\\frac{x - 3}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066859 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arcsin (\\frac{x + 3}{3}) + C" "\\frac{1}{3}\\arcsin \\left(\\frac{x + 3}{3}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066863 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x - 2}{\\sqrt{3}}) + C" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x - 2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066865 :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{x - 2}{3}) + C" "\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x - 2}{3}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066867 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x + 2}{\\sqrt{3}}) + C" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\arctan \\left(\\frac{x + 2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186066873 :answer/value "arcsec(\\frac{x - 2}{2}) + C" "arcsec\\left(\\frac{x - 2}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067433 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin (\\frac{2x + 1}{2}) + C" "\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin \\left(\\frac{2x + 1}{2}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186067434 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x +