e21bbfef14a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{x} + c$. Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve."] [:db/cas 17592186068364 :answer/value "y = (\\frac{x^{2} + x + C}{2})^{2}" "y = \\left(\\frac{x^{2} + x + C}{2}\\right)^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186068365 :answer/value "y = (\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2})^{2}" "y = \\left(\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2}\\right)^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186068456 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-5 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-5 & =-1 \\\\ k-5 & =-1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $y = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5.$" "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-5 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}-5 & =-1 \\\\ k-5 & =-1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $y = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068468 :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{a}{b-1})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{a}{b-1}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068494 :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z-2 & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}z & =5 \\\\ (\\frac{2}{7})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2}z) & =(\\frac{2}{7})\\cdot (5) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =\\frac{10}{7} \\\\ z & =\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{7}{2}z-2\\right),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ -(\\frac{7}{2}z-2) & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z+2 & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2}z & =1 \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{7})\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2}z) & =(-\\frac{2}{7})\\cdot (1) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z = -\\dfrac{2}{7}$ and $z = \\dfrac{10}{7}.$" "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z-2 & =3 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}z & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}z & =5 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2}z) & =\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (5) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =\\frac{10}{7} \\\\ z & =\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\left| \\dfrac{7}{2}z-2 \\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{7}{2}z-2\\right),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{2}z-2| & =3 \\\\ -(\\frac{7}{2}z-2) & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z+2 & =3 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\dfrac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2}z & =1 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2}z) & =\\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right)\\cdot (1) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{2}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{7}{2}}z) & =-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z = -\\dfrac{2}{7}$ and $z = \\dfrac{10}{7}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068504 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068507 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36(1-(3x-2)^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36(1-(3x-2)^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{6}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068563 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+4.$ Then $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral,  we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+4.$ Then $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral,  we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068567 :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068572 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068579 :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9}  = 1^3 - \\left( \\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{9}} & ={1}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9}  = 1^3 - \\left( \\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{9}} & ={1}^{3}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068580 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068586 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 & =A(2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)+B(2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 & =A(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)+B(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068587 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B .\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+3 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ 1 & =0+B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 3 & =A+B \\\\ 3 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2x+3}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}+{(-\\frac{1}{x+1})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1]-[\\frac{1}{2}-1] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B .\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+3 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ 1 & =0+B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 3 & =A+B \\\\ 3 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2x+3}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1]-[\\frac{1}{2}-1] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068588 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{A}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2x-1 & =A(3x-1)^{2}+B(3x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{3})-1 & =A{(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)}^{2}+B(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(0)}^{2}+2(0)-1 & =A{(0-1)}^{2}+B(0-1)+C \\\\ -1 & =A-B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-B & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get another equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(1)}^{2}+2(1)-1 & =A{(3(1)-1)}^{2}+B(3(1)-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A+2B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2A+B & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations $A-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$ and $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ 3A & =3 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+B & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ B & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{8}{3(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{3(3x-1)^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-1}\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|+\\frac{8}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3(3x-1)})+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{6(3x-1)^{2}})+K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|-\\frac{8}{9(3x-1)}-\\frac{1}{9(3x-1)^{2}}+K\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{A}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2x-1 & =A(3x-1)^{2}+B(3x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1 & =A{(3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1)}^{2}+B(3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-1)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(0)}^{2}+2(0)-1 & =A{(0-1)}^{2}+B(0-1)+C \\\\ -1 & =A-B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-B & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get another equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(1)}^{2}+2(1)-1 & =A{(3(1)-1)}^{2}+B(3(1)-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A+2B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2A+B & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations $A-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$ and $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ 3A & =3 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+B & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ B & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{8}{3(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{3(3x-1)^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-1}\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|+\\frac{8}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3(3x-1)})+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{6(3x-1)^{2}})+K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|-\\frac{8}{9(3x-1)}-\\frac{1}{9(3x-1)^{2}}+K\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068589 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{C}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=2,$ $B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{2}{x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+x+1.$ Then $(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{C}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=2,$ $B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{2}{x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+x+1.$ Then $(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068602 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x-3 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x-3 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186068614 :question/worked-solution "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6a\\cdot a\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}})+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$" "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}\\right)+6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}\\right)+6a\\cdot a\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}}\\right)+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}\\right) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068615 :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$" "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{2x}\\right) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{10x+7}{8x}\\right) & =8x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{2x}\\right) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}\\right) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}\\right) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068621 :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068622 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y }{\\textrm d x^2},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} ( \\sin 2t ) = 2 \\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /8} & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y }{\\textrm d x^2},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} ( \\sin 2t ) = 2 \\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /8} & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068628 :question/worked-solution "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068629 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{x}) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$" "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+2}{x}\\right) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068630 :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}})+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)+6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{6x}\\right) & =6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{3x}\\right) \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)+6x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{6x}\\right) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{3x}\\right) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}}\\right)+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}\\right) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186068632 :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=4e^{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4e^{2t}}{e^t} = 4e^t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=4e^{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4e^{2t}}{e^t} = 4e^t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068636 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068638 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve with the $y$-axis by solving for $y$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2 - y^{2})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π[4y - \\frac{4}{3}y^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}y^{5}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= 2π([4(\\sqrt{2}) - \\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt{2})^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}(\\sqrt{2})^{5}] - [0]) \\\\\n&= 2π(4\\sqrt{2} - \\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\\n&= 2π(\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64π\\sqrt{2}}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since the region is symmetrical about the $x$-axis, in step $3$ above we were able to write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\pi\\int_{-\\sqrt 2}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y = 2\\pi\\int_{0}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nThis step saved us a bit of work because the antiderivative evaluated to zero at the lower limit." "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve with the $y$-axis by solving for $y$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2 - y^{2})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4y^{2} + y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= 2π[4y - \\frac{4}{3}y^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}y^{5}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= 2π([4(\\sqrt{2}) - \\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt{2})^{3} + \\frac{1}{5}(\\sqrt{2})^{5}] - [0]) \\\\\n&= 2π(4\\sqrt{2} - \\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\\n&= 2π\\left(\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64π\\sqrt{2}}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since the region is symmetrical about the $x$-axis, in step $3$ above we were able to write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\pi\\int_{-\\sqrt 2}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y = 2\\pi\\int_{0}^\\sqrt 2 [g(y)]^2\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nThis step saved us a bit of work because the antiderivative evaluated to zero at the lower limit."] [:db/cas 17592186068644 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I\\mathbin.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I\\mathbin.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068659 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\cos(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So we have\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 1\n$$\n\nand multiplying both sides by $x^4$ gives\n\n$$\n-x^4 \\le x^4\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le x^4\n$$\n\nNow, taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-x^{4}) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos (\\frac{1}{x}) \\le lim_(x → 0)(x^{4}) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos (\\frac{1}{x}) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich, by the squeeze theorem, implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0} x^4\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = 0\n$$" "Recall that $\\cos(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So we have\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 1\n$$\n\nand multiplying both sides by $x^4$ gives\n\n$$\n-x^4 \\le x^4\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) \\le x^4\n$$\n\nNow, taking the limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-x^{4}) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(x^{4}) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{4}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich, by the squeeze theorem, implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0} x^4\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068665 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sin(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)}\\right|\\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\mid x^3 \\mid$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & \\cdot & \\sin (\\frac{3}{x}) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix};\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3}\\sin (\\frac{3}{x}) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nand using the definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n-\\begin{vmatrix}x^3 & \\leq x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} \\leq & x^3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we're using absolute value here because $x^3$ has an odd power.\n\nNow, taking the limit as $x\\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin (\\frac{3}{x}) \\le lim_(x → 0)(\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin (\\frac{3}{x}) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0} x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} = 0\n$$" "Recall that $\\sin(\\theta)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)}\\right|\\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\mid x^3 \\mid$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & \\cdot & \\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix};\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) & \\le & x^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nand using the definition of absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n-\\begin{vmatrix}x^3 & \\leq x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} \\leq & x^3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we're using absolute value here because $x^3$ has an odd power.\n\nNow, taking the limit as $x\\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le lim_(x → 0)(\\mid x^{3} \\mid) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)x^{3}\\sin \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the squeeze theorem, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0} x^3 \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x}\\right)} = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069024 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4((2x-3)^{2}/4+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x-3)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{2x-3}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-3}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4((2x-3)^{2}/4+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x-3)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-3}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247797 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{4})+C" "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{4}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186248694 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{2})+C" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186249126 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{2})+C" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x-3}{2}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186069033 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{9}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{2D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3}.$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{9}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{2D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069047 :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069068 :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069081 :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\frac{x}{x+1})\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\n$$" "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\left(\\frac{x}{x+1}\\right)\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069171 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-13/2$ | $-11/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-13/2$ | $-11/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069192 :question/problem "If the equation $\\log_{2} (\\frac{1}{8}) =-3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = a$, then $b =$ {{field-1}}." "If the equation $\\log_{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) =-3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = a$, then $b =$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186069196 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y = (\\frac{1}{5})^{x} + 4$?" "Which of the following is the graph of $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{x} + 4$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069197 :question/problem "![](images/60/60a01bfb824bb2aae025aacd937726ac786b3c69842baf619de94570157df252.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$" "![](images/60/60a01bfb824bb2aae025aacd937726ac786b3c69842baf619de94570157df252.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186069197 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$" "The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069198 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+4?$"] [:db/cas 17592186069198 :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1e/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/99/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)" "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1e/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/99/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186069199 :question/problem "![](images/76/768d0be7926a8f66d7b2ac193e842f7567d40e9c2217376b93ed284154520539.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" "![](images/76/768d0be7926a8f66d7b2ac193e842f7567d40e9c2217376b93ed284154520539.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069199 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4.$" "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{x}+4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069228 :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & <\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-10) & <3\\cdot (\\frac{6-2x}{3})<3\\cdot 0 \\\\ -30 & <6-2x<0 \\\\ -30-6 & <6-2x-6<0-6 \\\\ -36 & <-2x<-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-2.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & <-2x<-6 \\\\ \\frac{-36}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}>\\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\ 18 & >x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $18>x>3,$ but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n3<x<18\n$$" "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & <\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-10) & <3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{6-2x}{3}\\right)<3\\cdot 0 \\\\ -30 & <6-2x<0 \\\\ -30-6 & <6-2x-6<0-6 \\\\ -36 & <-2x<-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-2.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & <-2x<-6 \\\\ \\frac{-36}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}>\\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\ 18 & >x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $18>x>3,$ but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n3<x<18\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069229 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}} &= \\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{5})}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{2x}{5}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|2x|}{|5|} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5} &= \\frac{2\\cdot x}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}} &= \\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{2x}{5}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|2x|}{|5|} \\\\ &= \\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5} &= \\frac{2\\cdot x}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{2x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069234 :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." "For the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186069234 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186069235 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-2?$"] [:db/cas 17592186069235 :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/41/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)" "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/41/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186069247 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{x+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{(\\frac{1}{0+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069248 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069262 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡(x))(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069272 :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y &=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y &=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186069280 :question/worked-solution "We note that $b=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $36b$ and $12{b}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(36b,12{b}^{2})=36{b}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $36{b}^{2},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}} & =-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}} \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =b\\cdot 36b\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) & =b\\cdot \\cancel{36b}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{\\cancel{36b}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) \\\\ 3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8) \\\\ 15b+24 & =-12b+12b+24 \\\\ 15b+24 & =24 \\\\ 15b & =24-24 \\\\ 15b & =0 \\\\ b & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. Since $b=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution." "We note that $b=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $36b$ and $12{b}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(36b,12{b}^{2})=36{b}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $36{b}^{2},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}} & =-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}} \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}\\right) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}\\right) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{12}{36b}\\right)+36{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}\\right) & =b\\cdot 36b\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{12}{36b}\\right)+3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}\\right) & =b\\cdot \\cancel{36b}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{12}{\\cancel{36b}}\\right)+3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ 3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8) \\\\ 15b+24 & =-12b+12b+24 \\\\ 15b+24 & =24 \\\\ 15b & =24-24 \\\\ 15b & =0 \\\\ b & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. Since $b=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution."] [:db/cas 17592186069282 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+7 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+7 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+7 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+7 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+7 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+7 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321982 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{3})+C" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{3}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186322250 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186323136 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C"] [:db/cas 17592186069288 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-6)^{2}-4(2)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n15-25x=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=10,B=-15$ and $C=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}-6x+9| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9))-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-6)^{2}-4(2)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n15-25x=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=10,B=-15$ and $C=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}-6x+9| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9))-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069359 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x\\sin (\\frac{1}{x^{2}})$." "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186069360 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{2}\\cos (\\frac{1}{x})$." "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{2}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186069361 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{2}\\sin (\\frac{1}{x})$." "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{2}\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186069363 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{5}\\sin (\\frac{4}{x})$." "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)x^{5}\\sin \\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186069379 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-3)+B(3\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-3)+B(3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069382 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16=(x+4)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16} & =\\frac{3x+4}{(x+4)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+4=A(x-4)+B(x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)+4 & =A(4-4)+B(4+4) \\\\ 16 & =8B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+4 & =A(-4-4)+B(-4+4) \\\\ -8 & =-8A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}=\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-4)^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(24) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16=(x+4)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16} & =\\frac{3x+4}{(x+4)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+4=A(x-4)+B(x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)+4 & =A(4-4)+B(4+4) \\\\ 16 & =8B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+4 & =A(-4-4)+B(-4+4) \\\\ -8 & =-8A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}=\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-4)^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(24) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{24}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186330429 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{36})" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{36}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186330919 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24})" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{24}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186331907 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{32})" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{32}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186069397 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2=A({x}^{2}+1)+(Bx+C)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-1,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2=A({x}^{2}+1)+(Bx+C)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-1,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069399 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069401 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069412 :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,$ calculate $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" "Given that $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,$ calculate $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186069412 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2},\\,{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2},\\,{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069424 :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{4}{b})}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{4}{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\left(-\\frac{4}{b}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{b}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{b}\\right)} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(-\\frac{4}{b}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{4}{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069433 :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{2t}=4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{t}^{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t}{2t} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{2t}=4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{t}^{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t}{2t} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069442 :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d0b1cdb3ff99abaab113c5b0beaf0e1a3ad8738cbd84b1f95da5b7c1139582b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$  Find the value of $k-c.$" "![](images/2d/2d0b1cdb3ff99abaab113c5b0beaf0e1a3ad8738cbd84b1f95da5b7c1139582b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c.$  Find the value of $k-c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069442 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ hence $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =3 \\\\ k-3 & =3 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=6-(-3)=9.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=6{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$" "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ hence $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}-3 & =3 \\\\ k-3 & =3 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=6-(-3)=9.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=6{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069473 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0^{+})(\\ln x)/((\\frac{1}{x^{2}}))$." "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0^{+})(\\ln x)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right))$."] [:db/cas 17592186069480 :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{1/3} (\\frac{1}{9}) = 2$ is equivalent to $b^{x} = \\frac{1}{9}$ for some constants $b$ and $x$. What is the value of $x$?" "The equation $\\log_{1/3} \\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right) = 2$ is equivalent to $b^{x} = \\frac{1}{9}$ for some constants $b$ and $x$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069481 :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{3} (\\frac{1}{9}) =-2$ is equivalent to $b^{-2} = \\frac{1}{9}$. What is the value of $b$?" "The equation $\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right) =-2$ is equivalent to $b^{-2} = \\frac{1}{9}$. What is the value of $b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069515 :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x,2x,$ and $4x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,2x,4x)=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =2\\cdot 2x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{\\cancel{2x}})+\\cancel{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4x}}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (1) & =2\\cdot (2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2(2+x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =4+2x+3 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x,2x,$ and $4x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,2x,4x)=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} \\\\ 4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2+x}{2x}\\right)+4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4x}\\right) \\\\ 4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =2\\cdot 2x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2+x}{2x}\\right)+4x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4x}\\right) \\\\ 4\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2+x}{\\cancel{2x}}\\right)+\\cancel{4x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4x}}\\right) \\\\ 4\\cdot (1) & =2\\cdot (2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2(2+x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =4+2x+3 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186069532 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348602 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}" "y={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186350551 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2})}^{2}" "y={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186073607 :question/worked-solution "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nFirst, since $AD = 4$ and\n\n$$\nAD+DB=AB = 6\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nDB = AB-AD = 6-4=2\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the triangle proportionality theorem twice:\n\n- Since $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{AD}{DB} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Since $\\overline{EF} \\parallel \\overline{AB}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= \\frac{CE}{EA} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= (\\frac{AE}{EC})^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= 2^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nCF &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nFirst, since $AD = 4$ and\n\n$$\nAD+DB=AB = 6\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nDB = AB-AD = 6-4=2\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the triangle proportionality theorem twice:\n\n- Since $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{AD}{DB} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Since $\\overline{EF} \\parallel \\overline{AB}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= \\frac{CE}{EA} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= \\left(\\frac{AE}{EC}\\right)^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= 2^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nCF &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074258 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\tan 5^{∘} = \\tan (\\frac{π}{36})$\n2. $\\tan 10^{∘} = \\tan (\\frac{π}{18})$\n3. $\\tan 15^{∘} = \\tan (\\frac{π}{9})$" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\tan 5^{∘} = \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{36}\\right)$\n2. $\\tan 10^{∘} = \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{18}\\right)$\n3. $\\tan 15^{∘} = \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{9}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186074259 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\sin 20^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{π}{9})$\n2. $\\sin 50^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{2π}{9})$\n3. $\\sin 80^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{4π}{9})$" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\sin 20^{∘} = \\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{9}\\right)$\n2. $\\sin 50^{∘} = \\sin \\left(\\frac{2π}{9}\\right)$\n3. $\\sin 80^{∘} = \\sin \\left(\\frac{4π}{9}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186074260 :question/problem "What is $\\cos (\\frac{π}{3})$?" "What is $\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186074263 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186074263 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$" "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078147 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{4})=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k = 1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^k}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^{k+1}}\\right). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i =  \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}} & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ is true for all $n\\geq 1.$" "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k = 1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^k}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^{k+1}}\\right). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i =  \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}} & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ is true for all $n\\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078166 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3.$ We must make sure that *all* of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2n+6}{3} & =4n+1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2n+6}{3}) & =3\\cdot (4n+1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2n+6}{\\cancel{3}}) & =3\\cdot 4n+3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2n+6 & =12n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+6 & =12n+3 \\\\ 2n+6-2n & =12n+3-2n \\\\ 6 & =10n+3 \\\\ 6-3 & =10n+3-3 \\\\ 3 & =10n \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =\\frac{10n}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $n = \\dfrac{3}{10}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3.$ We must make sure that *all* of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2n+6}{3} & =4n+1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2n+6}{3}\\right) & =3\\cdot (4n+1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2n+6}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) & =3\\cdot 4n+3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2n+6 & =12n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+6 & =12n+3 \\\\ 2n+6-2n & =12n+3-2n \\\\ 6 & =10n+3 \\\\ 6-3 & =10n+3-3 \\\\ 3 & =10n \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =\\frac{10n}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $n = \\dfrac{3}{10}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078201 :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{16}{8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(-\\frac{1}{3})} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{27})}{(\\frac{1}{9})} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences." "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{16}{8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences."] [:db/cas 17592186078204 :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-2,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{6-1} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-32) \\\\ -\\frac{80}{32} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The common ratio is $r=-2,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{6-1} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-32) \\\\ -\\frac{80}{32} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078206 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{3}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1} \\\\ k-2 & =1 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{3}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-2} & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1} \\\\ k-2 & =1 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078239 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=49$, which gives\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(49) + f^{′}(49)(x - 49)\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(49)$ and $f'(49)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf\\left(49\\right) = \\sqrt{49}= 7\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(49\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{49}} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\cdot 7} = \\dfrac{1}{14}\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x) = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(x - 49)\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{49.1}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=49.1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} \\mid \\approx L(49.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(49.1 - 49); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(0.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(\\frac{1}{10}); = 7 + \\frac{1}{140}; = \\frac{981}{140}; \\approx 7.007143\n$$\n\nNote that the exact answer, rounded to six decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} = 7.007\\,139\n$$\n\nOur approximation is very close!" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=49$, which gives\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(49) + f^{′}(49)(x - 49)\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(49)$ and $f'(49)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf\\left(49\\right) = \\sqrt{49}= 7\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(49\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{49}} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\cdot 7} = \\dfrac{1}{14}\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x) = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(x - 49)\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{49.1}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=49.1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} \\mid \\approx L(49.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(49.1 - 49); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(0.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right); = 7 + \\frac{1}{140}; = \\frac{981}{140}; \\approx 7.007143\n$$\n\nNote that the exact answer, rounded to six decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} = 7.007\\,139\n$$\n\nOur approximation is very close!"] [:db/cas 17592186078241 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\tan x\n$$\n\nat\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sec^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(\\frac{π}{4}) + f^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2x + 1 - \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\tan x\n$$\n\nat\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sec^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + f^{′}\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2x + 1 - \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078242 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the derivative of any trigonometric function is always expressed in radians. For that reason, we need to convert all degree units to radians.\n\nTo convert an angle expressed in degrees to an equivalent angle in radians, we multiply the angle in degrees by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThis gives,\n\n$$\n90^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n89^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na = 90^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) +f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\cos x) = -\\sin x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 + (-1)(x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\cos{89^{\\circ}}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\na = 89^{\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos 89^{∘} \\mid \\approx L(\\frac{89π}{180}); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{89π}{180}; = \\frac{π}{180}\n$$" "Remember that the derivative of any trigonometric function is always expressed in radians. For that reason, we need to convert all degree units to radians.\n\nTo convert an angle expressed in degrees to an equivalent angle in radians, we multiply the angle in degrees by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThis gives,\n\n$$\n90^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n89^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na = 90^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) +f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\cos x) = -\\sin x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 + (-1)(x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\cos{89^{\\circ}}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\na = 89^{\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos 89^{∘} \\mid \\approx L\\left(\\frac{89π}{180}\\right); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{89π}{180}; = \\frac{π}{180}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078248 :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =6+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 0 \\right)$ and its radius is $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =6+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 0 \\right)$ and its radius is $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078265 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos.$ So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{K}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡L & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos.$ So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{K}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡L & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078332 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078336 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-5x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+9\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-5x\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078343 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078345 :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$" "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078347 :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$" "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{1}}{3{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078348 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(7)}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{28}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{24}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(7)}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{28}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{24}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."] [:db/cas 17592186078357 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(\\frac{π}{2}) + f^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})(x - \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} =0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x) =-\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\\right)\n$$\n\nby evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos (\\frac{7π}{12}) \\mid \\approx L(\\frac{7π}{12}); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{7π}{12}; =-\\frac{π}{12}\n$$" "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) + f^{′}\\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)(x - \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} =0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x) =-\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\\right)\n$$\n\nby evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\frac{7π}{12}\\right) \\mid \\approx L\\left(\\frac{7π}{12}\\right); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{7π}{12}; =-\\frac{π}{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078743 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."] [:db/cas 17592186078799 :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$" "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{256}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078840 :question/problem "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\arccos (\\frac{1}{4})$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\arccos \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186078841 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arctan (\\frac{1}{8})$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Find the value of $\\arctan \\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186078882 :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{4}{5})^{-1}$?" "What is $\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{-1}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186078918 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8y+5}{3} & =6y \\\\ 3(\\frac{8y+5}{3}) & =3\\cdot 6y \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{8y+5}{\\cancel{3}}) & =18y \\\\ 8y+5 & =18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y+5 & =18y \\\\ 8y+5-8y & =18y-8y \\\\ 5 & =10y \\\\ \\frac{5}{10} & =\\frac{10y}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8y+5}{3} & =6y \\\\ 3\\left(\\frac{8y+5}{3}\\right) & =3\\cdot 6y \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8y+5}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) & =18y \\\\ 8y+5 & =18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y+5 & =18y \\\\ 8y+5-8y & =18y-8y \\\\ 5 & =10y \\\\ \\frac{5}{10} & =\\frac{10y}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078933 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078994 :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\sin (\\frac{31π}{180})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x) = \\sin x$ at $x = \\frac{π}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Approximate the value of $\\sin \\left(\\frac{31π}{180}\\right)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x) = \\sin x$ at $x = \\frac{π}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186078999 :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\sin (\\frac{7π}{9})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x) = \\sin x$ at $x = \\frac{3π}{4}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Approximate the value of $\\sin \\left(\\frac{7π}{9}\\right)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x) = \\sin x$ at $x = \\frac{3π}{4}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186079019 :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population is equal to ${p}^{′}(t).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2t)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2t\\cdot {2}^{{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population is equal to ${p}^{′}(t).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2t)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2t\\cdot {2}^{{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079020 :question/problem "![](images/94/9484d0e553cbd2372cf589f79fec04f589d209b41c153a03f2cc1d21b96653df.png)\n\nThe plot above shows the graph of $y = \\sin x$ and its tangent line at $x = \\frac{3π}{4}$. What value gives the tangent line approximation of $\\sin (\\frac{5π}{8})$?" "![](images/94/9484d0e553cbd2372cf589f79fec04f589d209b41c153a03f2cc1d21b96653df.png)\n\nThe plot above shows the graph of $y = \\sin x$ and its tangent line at $x = \\frac{3π}{4}$. What value gives the tangent line approximation of $\\sin \\left(\\frac{5π}{8}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186079021 :question/problem "![](images/40/404e72ecf4edcef45aba1d1ffee484e8b3ce584c2177f1c983bd5586b89d36c1.png)\n\nThe plot above shows the graph of $y = \\cos x$ and its tangent line at $x =-\\frac{π}{6}$. What value gives the tangent line approximation of $\\cos (-\\frac{π}{12})$?" "![](images/40/404e72ecf4edcef45aba1d1ffee484e8b3ce584c2177f1c983bd5586b89d36c1.png)\n\nThe plot above shows the graph of $y = \\cos x$ and its tangent line at $x =-\\frac{π}{6}$. What value gives the tangent line approximation of $\\cos \\left(-\\frac{π}{12}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186079055 :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 2)^(∞)5(\\frac{3}{2})^{n}$\n2. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{2}{3})^{n}$\n3. $∑_(n = 3)^(∞)\\frac{4^{n + 2}}{2^{n - 1}}$" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 2)^(∞)5\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}$\n2. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n}$\n3. $∑_(n = 3)^(∞)\\frac{4^{n + 2}}{2^{n - 1}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186079056 :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 6)^(∞)(0.6)(-0.7)^{n}$\n2. $∑_(n = 4)^(∞)(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{5}{3})^{n}$\n3. $∑_(n = 7)^(∞)\\frac{4^{2n}}{8^{n + 1}}$" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 6)^(∞)(0.6)(-0.7)^{n}$\n2. $∑_(n = 4)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{n}$\n3. $∑_(n = 7)^(∞)\\frac{4^{2n}}{8^{n + 1}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186079058 :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{3^{k}}{27})^{n}$ converge?" "For which values of $k$ does the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{3^{k}}{27}\\right)^{n}$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186079067 :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-3y) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$" "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-3y) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}) & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186079114 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that ${a}_{3}=12$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that the sequence is positive.\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that ${a}_{k}=48:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & =\\frac{48}{12} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & =4 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2} \\\\ k-3 & =-2 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that ${a}_{3}=12$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that the sequence is positive.\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that ${a}_{k}=48:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-3} & =\\frac{48}{12} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-3} & =4 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{k-3} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-2} \\\\ k-3 & =-2 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079123 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $∑_(n = 2)^(∞)(\\frac{1}{4})^{n}$?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $∑_(n = 2)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{n}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186079165 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079167 :question/problem "![](images/36/36825a025fe05429a34c3a364c6a20e6d8b5a18da32a53abe46f9b3790520552.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (\\frac{3x}{2}) + D$, where $D$ is a constant. What is the value of $D$?" "![](images/36/36825a025fe05429a34c3a364c6a20e6d8b5a18da32a53abe46f9b3790520552.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan \\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right) + D$, where $D$ is a constant. What is the value of $D$?"] [:db/cas 17592186079193 :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{3}{2})^{-1}$?" "What is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{-1}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186079203 :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+{y}^{2}-2y & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}) & =\\frac{7}{4}+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {(x-(-\\frac{5}{2}))}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{5}{2},1)$ and its radius is $3.$" "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+{y}^{2}-2y & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}) & =\\frac{7}{4}+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right))}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{5}{2},1)$ and its radius is $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186080034 :answer/value "∑_(n = 1)^(∞)88.1(\\frac{1}{10})^{n}" "∑_(n = 1)^(∞)88.1\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)^{n}"] [:db/cas 17592186080035 :answer/value "∑_(n = 1)^(∞)881(\\frac{1}{100})^{n}" "