 by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$" "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186087456 :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087458 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186087474 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{2}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{2}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087475 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{2\\theta}}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $\\sin{2\\theta}$ and then compute its reciprocal.\n\nTo find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt 3}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 2θ &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 2θ} = (1)/((-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{2\\theta}}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $\\sin{2\\theta}$ and then compute its reciprocal.\n\nTo find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt 3}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 2θ &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 2θ} = (1)/(\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)) =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087476 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta > 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta > 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087477 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087478 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087483 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the given expression by $(-1)$ and rewrite it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(\\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5}) - 1) &= (-2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(1 - \\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5})) \\\\\n&= - (2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(1 - \\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1 - \\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1-\\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)} = -\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\right)\n$$" "First, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the given expression by $(-1)$ and rewrite it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right))/(\\tan^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right) - 1) &= (-2\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right))/(1 - \\tan^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right)) \\\\\n&= - (2\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right))/(1 - \\tan^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{5}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1 - \\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1-\\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)} = -\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087487 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\ninto our expression and then simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5\\tan 2x}{\\cot x - \\tan x} &= (5\\tan 2x)/((\\frac{1}{\\tan x} - \\tan x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5\\tan 2x \\cdot \\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x} \\\\\n&= 5\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan {2x} = \\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}) &= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\tan 2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan^{2} 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\tan{2x}}{\\cot{x}-\\tan{x}} = \\dfrac{5}{2} \\tan^2 {2x}\n$$" "Substituting\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\ninto our expression and then simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5\\tan 2x}{\\cot x - \\tan x} &= (5\\tan 2x)/((\\frac{1}{\\tan x} - \\tan x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5\\tan 2x \\cdot \\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x} \\\\\n&= 5\\tan 2x \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan {2x} = \\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\tan 2x \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}\\right) &= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\tan 2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan^{2} 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\tan{2x}}{\\cot{x}-\\tan{x}} = \\dfrac{5}{2} \\tan^2 {2x}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087490 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan 4θ = \\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan 2\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ} &= 4 \\cdot (\\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}) = 4\\tan 4θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\tan{2\\theta}}{1-\\tan^2{2\\theta}} = 4\\tan{4\\theta}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan 4θ = \\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan 2\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ} &= 4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}\\right) = 4\\tan 4θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\tan{2\\theta}}{1-\\tan^2{2\\theta}} = 4\\tan{4\\theta}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087519 :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z = -5+12\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(-5)}^{2}+{(12)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = -5$ is negative and $y = 12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the second quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{12}{-5}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.966,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z = -5 + 12\\textrm{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = 13(\\cos(1.966)+\\textrm{i}\\sin(1.966))\n$$" "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z = -5+12\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(-5)}^{2}+{(12)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = -5$ is negative and $y = 12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the second quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{12}{-5}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.966,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z = -5 + 12\\textrm{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = 13(\\cos(1.966)+\\textrm{i}\\sin(1.966))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087520 :question/worked-solution "We will simplify the right-hand side. Since $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will simplify the right-hand side. Since $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087521 :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\textrm{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\textrm{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $2+\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(2+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = \\dfrac{6}{5}$ and $y = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0.464,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$" "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\textrm{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\textrm{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $2+\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(2+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = \\dfrac{6}{5}$ and $y = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.464,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087541 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta = \\arg(z),$ we first calculate $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into $z=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ we get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta = \\arg(z),$ we first calculate $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into $z=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ we get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087557 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087559 :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$(t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2$.\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$(3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/((\\frac{1}{3})); = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$." "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$(t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2$.\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$(3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)); = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$."] [:db/cas 17592186087572 :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = \\dfrac{b}{a}$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x & =0 \\\\ 4 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p\\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{4}{3}) & =-6{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+11(\\frac{4}{3})-4 \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{44}{3}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+44-12}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0,$ so $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$" "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = \\dfrac{b}{a}$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x & =0 \\\\ 4 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p\\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) & =-6{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+11\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-4 \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{44}{3}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+44-12}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0,$ so $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186087573 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<      \\arg(z)<      \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})≈1.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<      \\arg(z)<      \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)≈1.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186087580 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186087589 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$" "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087628 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{\\left(\\frac{2}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087635 :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\ln(1+x),$ we use the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\ln{(1+2x)},$ we replace $x$ with $2x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x) & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the two series to give the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) \\\\  & =(2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯)-(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the series $\\ln(1+2x),$ the radius of convergence is given by\n\n$$\n-1 < 2x \\leq 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right].\n$$\n\nThe series $\\ln(1+x)$ has a radius of convergence of $x\\in(-1,1].$ Therefore, the radius of convergence of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] \\cap (-1,1] = \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] .\n$$" "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\ln(1+x),$ we use the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\ln{(1+2x)},$ we replace $x$ with $2x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x) & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the two series to give the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) \\\\  & =(2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯)-(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the series $\\ln(1+2x),$ the radius of convergence is given by\n\n$$\n-1 < 2x \\leq 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right].\n$$\n\nThe series $\\ln(1+x)$ has a radius of convergence of $x\\in(-1,1].$ Therefore, the radius of convergence of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] \\cap (-1,1] = \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186087637 :question/worked-solution "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\dfrac{2}{3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f({\\color{blue}x}) = \\dfrac{1}{1-{\\color{blue}x}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ 3 & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $3.$" "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\dfrac{2}{3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}+⋯+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f({\\color{blue}x}) = \\dfrac{1}{1-{\\color{blue}x}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}+⋯+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ 3 & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186087638 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = \\ln(1+x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\frac{2}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right).$" "Notice that we can rewrite the given sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = \\ln(1+x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\frac{2}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186087643 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\gt 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\gt 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088008 :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ \\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272978 :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{{v}^{2}+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186273285 :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186273437 :answer/value "(\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" "\\left(\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x"] [:db/cas 17592186273999 :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x"] [:db/cas 17592186274558 :answer/value "(\\frac{{v}^{2}+v}{3{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\left(\\frac{{v}^{2}+v}{3{v}^{2}-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186088057 :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =4+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.644.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=5$ and $\\theta =0.644$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =4+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.644.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=5$ and $\\theta =0.644$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088058 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{-1})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈2.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =2.034$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{-1}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈2.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =2.034$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088065 :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}x+y=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}x+y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}x+y) & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 2x+10y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+10y=5.\n$$" "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}x+y=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}x+y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}x+y) & =10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2x+10y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+10y=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088094 :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186088094 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288092 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288272 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288476 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186288584 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186289191 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186088095 :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186088095 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288815 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186289086 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186289165 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186289533 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186289548 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186088096 :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$" "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186088096 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088122 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{9}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{81}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088189 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}.$ The definition of ${y}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(5(x+h)-4(x+h)^{2})-(5x-4{x}^{2})}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5x+5h-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}-5x+4{x}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(5-8x-4h)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(5-8x-4h) \\\\  & =5-8x-4(0) \\\\  & =5-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}.$ The definition of ${y}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(5(x+h)-4(x+h)^{2})-(5x-4{x}^{2})}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{5x+5h-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}-5x+4{x}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h(5-8x-4h)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(5-8x-4h) \\\\  & =5-8x-4(0) \\\\  & =5-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088256 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{z}+7 & =5 \\\\ -4{e}^{z} & =-2 \\\\ {e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{z}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$" "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{z}+7 & =5 \\\\ -4{e}^{z} & =-2 \\\\ {e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{z}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ z & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186088257 :question/problem "What is $2\\ln (\\frac{1}{e})$?" "What is $2\\ln \\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186088287 :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088288 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088323 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{49}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088410 :question/problem "Express $z = 3\\sqrt{2}(\\cos (\\frac{5π}{4}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{5π}{4}))$ in the form $z = x + \\textrm{i}y$." "Express $z = 3\\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{5π}{4}\\right))$ in the form $z = x + \\textrm{i}y$."] [:db/cas 17592186088411 :question/problem "Express $z = 2(\\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{π}{3}))$ in the form $z = x + \\textrm{i}y$." "Express $z = 2(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right))$ in the form $z = x + \\textrm{i}y$."] [:db/cas 17592186088430 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=2pt \\\\ -3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=2pt \\\\ -3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)t,\\,y=\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088432 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{\\left(-\\frac{3}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088434 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186088439 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?"] [:db/cas 17592186088439 :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{vx})+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{vx}\\right)+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351700 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186352523 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\frac{1}{2v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186353123 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x"] [:db/cas 17592186353410 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1"] [:db/cas 17592186353422 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" "\\frac{1}{v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}"] [:db/cas 17592186088445 :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that a