iable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{5}|<1.$" "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{5}|<1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186380299 :answer/value 